Specialization could be seen as a good thing in some cases. 

Specialization is the focus of resources on one good.

Opportunity cost is defined as the cost of choosing one thing to do, measured in
the loss of another good that you would need to give up. The opportunity cost of
specialization is the other goods that aren't being produced due to the focus of
those resources on one good.

For example, 75% of all cars manufactured in the United States were produced in 
Detroit by the 1950s. 

This means that the specialization of resources was good for Detroit at first - the
focus of resources resulted in an increase in the cars manufactured in Detroit, which
means that the resources in Detroit were being used efficiently relative to the cars
produced.

This resulted in Detroit generating enormous wealth because they were so specialized.
